# Flutter Component Revision Page 8 — `Center`

## 1. What is `Center`?

`Center` is a Flutter layout widget used to **place its child exactly in the center of the available space**.

### Simple meaning

> `Center` = Put the child in the middle.

Example:

```dart
Center(
  child: Text("Hello"),
)
```

The `Text` will appear in the center of the available area.

---

## 2. Basic Structure

```dart
Center(
  child: Widget(),
)
```

`Center` accepts **one child**.

Example:

```dart
Center(
  child: Text("Welcome"),
)
```

---

## 3. Complete Example

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(
    MaterialApp(
      home: Scaffold(
        appBar: AppBar(
          title: Text("Center Example"),
        ),
        body: Center(
          child: Text(
            "Hello Flutter",
            style: TextStyle(
              fontSize: 24,
            ),
          ),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  );
}
```

### Result

The text appears in the **center of the body**.

---

# 4. Why do we need `Center`?

Suppose you write:

```dart
Scaffold(
  body: Text("Hello"),
)
```

The text normally starts near the **top-left** of the available body.

If you want it in the center:

```dart
Scaffold(
  body: Center(
    child: Text("Hello"),
  ),
)
```

So `Center` controls the **position of its child**.

---

# 5. `Center` with Different Widgets

`Center` can contain almost any widget.

### Text

```dart
Center(
  child: Text("Hello"),
)
```

### Icon

```dart
Center(
  child: Icon(Icons.home),
)
```

### Button

```dart
Center(
  child: ElevatedButton(
    onPressed: () {},
    child: Text("Login"),
  ),
)
```

### Container

```dart
Center(
  child: Container(
    width: 200,
    height: 100,
    color: Colors.blue,
  ),
)
```

The entire `Container` will be centered.

---

# 6. Centering a Container

This is very common when creating login screens or cards.

```dart
Scaffold(
  body: Center(
    child: Container(
      width: 300,
      height: 200,
      color: Colors.blue,
      child: Center(
        child: Text(
          "Login",
          style: TextStyle(
            color: Colors.white,
            fontSize: 24,
          ),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  ),
)
```

There are **two Centers** here.

```text
Center
   ↓
Container
   ↓
Center
   ↓
Text
```

### First `Center`

Centers the `Container` on the screen.

### Second `Center`

Centers the `Text` inside the `Container`.

---

# 7. `Center` vs `Padding`

These widgets do different jobs.

### `Center`

Controls **position**.

```dart
Center(
  child: Text("Hello"),
)
```

Meaning:

> Put Hello in the center.

### `Padding`

Controls **space around the child**.

```dart
Padding(
  padding: EdgeInsets.all(20),
  child: Text("Hello"),
)
```

Meaning:

> Give 20 pixels of space around Hello.

---

# 8. `Center` vs `Container(alignment:)`

You can also center a child using `Container`.

```dart
Container(
  alignment: Alignment.center,
  child: Text("Hello"),
)
```

This produces a similar result to:

```dart
Center(
  child: Text("Hello"),
)
```

### Difference

`Center` is specifically designed for centering.

`Container` can do many other things such as:

* background color
* padding
* margin
* border
* border radius
* shadow
* alignment

So if you only need centering:

```dart
Center(
  child: Text("Hello"),
)
```

is simpler.

---

# 9. Centering Multiple Widgets

`Center` accepts only **one child**.

This is ❌ wrong:

```dart
Center(
  children: [
    Text("Name"),
    Text("Email"),
  ],
)
```

Instead, put a `Column` inside `Center`.

```dart
Center(
  child: Column(
    mainAxisSize: MainAxisSize.min,
    children: [
      Text("Name"),
      Text("Email"),
    ],
  ),
)
```

Structure:

```text
Center
   ↓
Column
   ↓
 ┌─────────┐
 │ Name    │
 │ Email   │
 └─────────┘
```

The `Center` centers the entire `Column`.

---

# 10. `mainAxisSize: MainAxisSize.min`

This is important when using `Column` inside `Center`.

```dart
Center(
  child: Column(
    mainAxisSize: MainAxisSize.min,
    children: [
      Text("Welcome"),
      SizedBox(height: 10),
      ElevatedButton(
        onPressed: () {},
        child: Text("Login"),
      ),
    ],
  ),
)
```

`MainAxisSize.min` tells the `Column`:

> Take only as much vertical space as your children need.

This is useful for small groups of widgets that you want centered together.

---

# 11. Real-World Example — Login Screen

A common login screen might use:

```dart
Scaffold(
  body: Center(
    child: Column(
      mainAxisSize: MainAxisSize.min,
      children: [
        Text(
          "Login",
          style: TextStyle(
            fontSize: 30,
            fontWeight: FontWeight.bold,
          ),
        ),

        SizedBox(height: 20),

        TextField(
          decoration: InputDecoration(
            hintText: "Email",
          ),
        ),

        SizedBox(height: 10),

        TextField(
          decoration: InputDecoration(
            hintText: "Password",
          ),
        ),

        SizedBox(height: 20),

        ElevatedButton(
          onPressed: () {},
          child: Text("Login"),
        ),
      ],
    ),
  ),
)
```

Here:

```text
Center
   ↓
Column
   ↓
Login
   ↓
Email
   ↓
Password
   ↓
Login Button
```

The whole group is centered.

---

# 12. Where is `Center` commonly used?

You will frequently use `Center` for:

* Login screens
* Loading indicators
* Empty screens
* Error messages
* Welcome screens
* Splash screens
* Empty shopping carts
* Empty music playlists
* No-data messages
* Centered buttons
* Centered icons
* Centered cards

Example:

```dart
Center(
  child: CircularProgressIndicator(),
)
```

This displays the loading indicator in the center.

---

# 13. Important Point

`Center` does **not** mean the child itself becomes bigger.

For example:

```dart
Center(
  child: Text("Hello"),
)
```

The `Text` remains only as large as necessary.

`Center` simply positions it in the center.

---

# 14. Mental Model

Think of the screen as a large room:

```text
┌───────────────────────────┐
│                           │
│                           │
│          Hello            │
│                           │
│                           │
└───────────────────────────┘
```

`Center` tells Flutter:

> "Take my child and place it in the middle of my available space."

---

# 15. Common Mistakes

### Mistake 1 — Using `children`

❌

```dart
Center(
  children: [
    Text("A"),
    Text("B"),
  ],
)
```

`Center` accepts only:

```dart
child:
```

Use a `Column` or `Row` for multiple widgets.

---

### Mistake 2 — Expecting Center to arrange multiple widgets

For multiple widgets:

```dart
Center(
  child: Column(
    children: [
      Text("A"),
      Text("B"),
    ],
  ),
)
```

`Center` centers the **Column**, while the `Column` arranges the children.

---

# 16. Quick Revision

| Widget      | Main purpose                               |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| `Center`    | Center one child                           |
| `Padding`   | Add space around a child                   |
| `SizedBox`  | Give fixed size / spacing                  |
| `Container` | Box with size, decoration, alignment, etc. |
| `Row`       | Arrange widgets horizontally               |
| `Column`    | Arrange widgets vertically                 |

---

## One-Line Definition

> **`Center` is a Flutter layout widget that positions its single child at the center of the available space.**

### Basic syntax to remember

```dart
Center(
  child: YourWidget(),
)
```
